WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:05.630
Film Courage: Lee let’s talk about writing
partnerships…is it just like finding a good

00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:06.630
roommate?

00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:07.950
Is it nothing like that?

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:08.950
Lee Jessup: No.

00:00:08.950 --> 00:00:11.820
It’s like buying a house together and committing
to living in it for the rest of your life.

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:12.880
Film Courage: Hhmmm.

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:18.800
Okay… Lee Jessup: Writing partnerships are
mostly tragic.

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:19.930
They mostly end in divorce.

00:00:19.930 --> 00:00:25.109
You know I do work with a couple of partnerships
that have been consistent through the years

00:00:25.109 --> 00:00:30.511
that have been successful but it’s two partnerships
that have lasted from the dozens that I’ve

00:00:30.511 --> 00:00:31.640
seen break up over the years.

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:34.370
Some of them were quite successful together.

00:00:34.370 --> 00:00:38.140
So if you chose to participate in a writing
partnership you have to really understand

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:40.450
what it means to you, to your body of work.

00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:46.690
Effectively the moment that you hit with a
script written with a partnership with another

00:00:46.690 --> 00:00:51.990
person, everything else that will be expected
of you will be expected in corporation, in

00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:57.059
partnership with another person because you
and this other person have been able to create

00:00:57.059 --> 00:01:01.840
the secret sauce that made a great script,
that birthed a beautiful child (if you will).

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:05.799
And so when you consider writing partnerships
you really want to consider what it means

00:01:05.799 --> 00:01:07.579
for your body of work.

00:01:07.579 --> 00:01:11.810
Are you willing to discard your previous body
of work or not doing anything with it should

00:01:11.810 --> 00:01:13.859
you hit in a partnership.

00:01:13.859 --> 00:01:18.479
Are you willing to write more than one project
with that partner.

00:01:18.479 --> 00:01:22.119
Because the agent or manager is now going
to take on the partnership, not an individual

00:01:22.119 --> 00:01:23.119
writer.

00:01:23.119 --> 00:01:27.009
Oftentimes they don’t want to get involved
in the drama of the partnership breaking up.

00:01:27.009 --> 00:01:28.070
That can be very unattractive.

00:01:28.070 --> 00:01:33.790
I have a TV writing partnership that just
broke up where the agent told the two writers

00:01:33.790 --> 00:01:39.389
who broke up “Either I take both of you
on individually or I take neither one of you

00:01:39.389 --> 00:01:40.919
on individually.”

00:01:40.919 --> 00:01:45.789
So even broken up, the writers had to make
sure the other impressed in order to keep

00:01:45.789 --> 00:01:48.700
the agent for both of them or else they both
lose.

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:52.271
Because the agent just didn’t want to get
involved in the politics and didn’t want

00:01:52.271 --> 00:01:54.229
to have to choose.

00:01:54.229 --> 00:01:58.979
So you really have to consider whether you
and the writing partner want to write the

00:01:58.979 --> 00:02:01.659
same type of material, if you have the same
vision.

00:02:01.659 --> 00:02:06.099
So if one want to write film and the other
wants to write TV and that’s all you want

00:02:06.099 --> 00:02:07.289
to write, that’s going to be a problem.

00:02:07.289 --> 00:02:12.540
The only way you can really vacillate on a
partnership is if you’re writing together

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:16.129
on television but you’re writing alone on
film.

00:02:16.129 --> 00:02:19.260
Then you have a little more wiggle room.

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:26.540
But in general I find that reps either want
to rep the teams or the individual, not both.

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:34.670
It’s the same with producers who want to
work with the team who brought them a beautiful

00:02:34.670 --> 00:02:37.310
previous project.

00:02:37.310 --> 00:02:43.040
So you really have to consider what the partnership
is, what the partners bring into the partnership.

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:47.930
So you really want to think about how does
the partnership balance out.

00:02:47.930 --> 00:02:50.409
Are we good for the distribution of work?

00:02:50.409 --> 00:02:52.720
Are we going to be good for the next 10 years?

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:57.090
If one writer is going the heavy lifting and
the other writer is just giving notes are

00:02:57.090 --> 00:03:01.299
you going to feel okay about it in 5 years
when your writing partners is collecting your

00:03:01.299 --> 00:03:05.079
money just for giving you notes?

00:03:05.079 --> 00:03:09.810
Do you have an idea of what you want to write
in 10 years in terms of genre, in terms of

00:03:09.810 --> 00:03:12.150
the space you want to be in?

00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:13.930
All of those things need to be thought through.

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:20.370
You need to get the terms of the partnership
on paper because the truth of the matter is,

00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:21.629
most partnerships do go south.

00:03:21.629 --> 00:03:25.930
Film Courage: I’m thinking of what I call
the “Two Steves” (Apple’s Wozniak and

00:03:25.930 --> 00:03:26.930
Jobs).

00:03:26.930 --> 00:03:30.409
So possible if it was two Steve Jobs, it would
not have worked.

00:03:30.409 --> 00:03:35.590
But because they both (Jobs and Wozniak) had
these two different skill sets, it worked.

00:03:35.590 --> 00:03:39.159
And so two writers almost have to be polar
opposites for it to work?

00:03:39.159 --> 00:03:40.719
Lee Jessup: They have to compliment one another.

00:03:40.719 --> 00:03:43.780
They have to supplement where the other is
weaker.

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:46.099
They have to bring the best out of each other.

00:03:46.099 --> 00:03:47.480
They have to push each other.

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:50.370
They have to be able to fight.

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:54.579
Certainly you don’t want to writers that
do the exact same thing together.

00:03:54.579 --> 00:04:00.310
Because then is the partnership the sum of
its parts or is it less than that?

00:04:00.310 --> 00:04:05.219
So you want to compliment one another where
the other is weak.

00:04:05.219 --> 00:04:07.269
Film Courage: Right…Interesting.

00:04:07.269 --> 00:04:10.879
Do you think people really know that when
they first meet someone?

00:04:10.879 --> 00:04:12.110
Lee Jessup: No!

00:04:12.110 --> 00:04:17.660
A lot of writers that I work with tend to
look to writing partnerships because they

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:23.639
don’t want to write alone anymore because
it’s just comforting to have someone waiting

00:04:23.639 --> 00:04:30.180
for pages on the other end, because they’re
finding it tougher and tougher for them to

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:34.699
try to push a script through, why don’t
I do it with a writing partner?

00:04:34.699 --> 00:04:39.610
I find that most writers go into a partnership
that way without taking a step back and saying

00:04:39.610 --> 00:04:45.040
“Am I really ready to step into a creative
marriage here?”

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:46.040
Some of them do.

00:04:46.040 --> 00:04:47.590
Most of them don’t.

00:04:47.590 --> 00:04:53.910
I’ve had partnerships that have sold multi-million
dollar scripts and then broke up because the

00:04:53.910 --> 00:04:55.470
relationship was so difficult.

00:04:55.470 --> 00:04:56.889
Film Courage: Why at that point?

00:04:56.889 --> 00:05:00.080
Lee Jessup: Because the writers can stand
each other at that point because they hate

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:04.470
each other because they couldn’t think less
of each other’s creative talents.

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:09.610
Because it was never a positive relationship
to start with.

00:05:09.610 --> 00:05:15.410
I had one relationship that had a feature
film get greenlit where the two writers just

00:05:15.410 --> 00:05:16.410
could not get along.

00:05:16.410 --> 00:05:20.890
One was incredibly abusive and condescending
and kept insisting that he was doing all of

00:05:20.890 --> 00:05:23.690
the work when the other was really doing the
brunt of the work.

00:05:23.690 --> 00:05:27.550
And they just didn’t want to go through
that again.

00:05:27.550 --> 00:05:32.310
So I’ve seen writing partnerships break
up with a lot to lose.

00:05:32.310 --> 00:05:35.379
And I think it’s not for nothing, like I
said I’ve also seen writing partnerships

00:05:35.379 --> 00:05:40.590
succeed but I think it’s where the partnerships
come together with a point of view of doing

00:05:40.590 --> 00:05:44.770
this from the long haul as opposed to let’s
try it out.

00:05:44.770 --> 00:05:51.670
Really thinking about it as a long term relationship
as opposed to “We’ll test the waters and

00:05:51.670 --> 00:05:52.289
then consider.”

